2606.13178,2606.13178,"12,13",cross_page,"At α = 0.23 in the learned bottleneck experiment, what is the observed difference in negative log likelihood (NLL) between the frozen hinge bottleneck and the full-input log-loss learner, and what does this difference represent according to the theoretical framework discussed?","The frozen hinge bottleneck has an NLL of 0.516 and the full-input log-loss learner has an NLL of 0.499, so the difference is approximately 0.017 nats; this difference represents the log-loss gap equal to the conditional information discarded by the frozen representation.","The frozen hinge bottleneck achieves NLL 0.516, corresponding to an additional penalty of approximately 0.017 nats relative to the full-input learner. Thus the learned bottleneck retains enough information for the source classification decision and remains incomplete for optimal probabilistic prediction after freezing.",The controlled experiment shows that a Bayes-minimal classification representation has a log-loss gap equal to the conditional information it discards.,"PAGE A provides the specific NLL values and their difference, while PAGE B explains that this difference represents the log-loss gap due to discarded conditional information.",pending,multihop-v1,0.471,0.000,needs_both_pages
2606.13282,2606.13282,"1,2",cross_page,"What is the dimensionality of the Ethical Consequence Space (ECS) used by ERTS, and what is the main reason for choosing this specific value over alternatives?","The ECS is 22-dimensional, chosen because lower dimensionality masked bias detection and higher dimensionality included variables with near-zero variance.","We present the Ethical Robustness Testing System (ERTS), addressing this gap through four contributions: 1) The Ethical Consequence Space (ECS): a 22- dimensional vector representation grounded in utilitar- ian, deontological, and virtue ethics frameworks, where each dimension corresponds to a named ethical variable with defined polarity and semantic meaning.","We settled on d = 22 after iterating between d = 15 and d = 25 during development. At d = 15, fairness and discrimination variables collapsed into a single factor that masked bias detection failures under authority injection pertur- bations, leading to false negatives. At d = 25, three variables (cultural_sensitivity, environmental_impact, intergenerational_harm) showed near-zero variance across our scenario corpus and were removed. The current dimensionality represents the minimal set that preserves dis- criminative power across all 7 perturbation categories.",PAGE A gives the dimensionality and existence of the ECS; PAGE B explains why 22 was chosen over 15 and 25.,pending,multihop-v1,0.269,0.540,needs_both_pages
2606.13282,2606.13282,"2,3",cross_page,"What is the minimum value of δmax required to ensure the feasibility of the constraint set C1–C6 for the ECS dimensionality used in ERTS, and what is the actual δmax value chosen in the configuration?","The minimum δmax required is greater than Bmin/d = 0.0023, and the actual δmax chosen is 0.5.",We settled on d = 22 after iterating between d = 15 and d = 25 during development. ... The current dimensionality represents the minimal set that preserves discriminative power across all 7 perturbation categories.,"The constraint set C1–C6 is feasible whenever Bmin < Bmax and δmax > Bmin/d. In our configuration (Bmin = 0.05, Bmax = 2.0, δmax = 0.5, d = 22), these conditions hold trivially (0.05 < 2.0 and 0.5 > 0.0023). Thus valid perturbations always exist.","PAGE A provides the ECS dimensionality d = 22, while PAGE B gives the feasibility condition δmax > Bmin/d and the actual δmax value used.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,0.828,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.83)"
2606.13282,2606.13282,"3,4",cross_page,What is the minimum required Ethical Robustness Score (ERS) and the maximum allowed percentage of critical failures for an AI system to be cleared for deployment in the Healthcare domain according to the ERTS framework?,ERS ≥ 0.85 and 0% critical failures.,"The Ethical Instability Index (EII) is a composite metric in [0, 1] quantifying how much an AI model’s ethical decision changed under perturbation. Given normal decision Dn and perturbed decision Dp: EII = w1Faction + w2Fconf + w3Fscore + w4Frank (9)","Healthcare 0.85 0.85 0.90 0 10% ... Healthcare thresholds (ERS ≥0.85, zero critical failures) are informed by the zero-tolerance-for-harm principle in biomedical ethics [30] and the reliability requirements analogous to IEC 61508 SIL-3 safety systems.","PAGE A defines the ERS metric and its role in ethical robustness, while PAGE B provides the specific Healthcare domain thresholds for ERS and critical failures.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,1.000,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=1.00)"
2606.13282,2606.13282,"4,5",cross_page,"According to the domain-specific assessment thresholds and the experimental results, which models achieved CLEARED status for all domains, and what is the maximum number of critical failures allowed for the Healthcare domain?",Gemini-2.0-Flash and RuleBased achieved CLEARED status for all domains; the maximum number of critical failures allowed for Healthcare is zero.,"Healthcare thresholds (ERS ≥0.85, zero critical failures) are informed by the zero-tolerance-for-harm principle in biomedical ethics [30] and the reliability requirements analogous to IEC 61508 SIL-3 safety systems.",Only 2 of 6 models (33%) achieved assessment clearance. Gemini-2.0-Flash cleared all domains with zero failures across 250 tests. The RuleBased baseline cleared all domains with only 9 moderate-severity failures and zero critical failures.,"PAGE A provides the Healthcare domain threshold for critical failures, while PAGE B identifies which models achieved CLEARED status for all domains.",pending,multihop-v1,0.000,0.000,needs_both_pages
2606.13285,2606.13285,"1,2",cross_page,"According to the definitions provided, what is the mathematical relationship between the change in proportion of one system and the changes in proportion of the other systems in the ensemble, and what does γi represent in this context?","The change in proportion in one system equals the negative sum of the changes in proportion from all other systems (∆γi = −∑_{j≠i} ∆γj), where γi is the proportion of system si to the ensemble M S (γi = si/M S).","By viewing interacting systems as components of a larger super-system, we can estimate their collective equilibrium state. This estimation reveals the tendencies of change across systems. From this perspective, the future states of all member systems within the ensemble can be predicted jointly.","Definition 2: When the ensemble of multiple systems M S is viewed in its entirety, the change in proportion in one system complements the total changes in proportion from other systems.
