2606.13287,2606.13287,"1,2",cross_page,"According to the authors, what is the main theoretical benefit of using gradient clipping in asynchronous SGD for federated learning with heterogeneous data, and how is this reflected in the convergence rate?","Clipped ASGD achieves convergence rates that do not depend on the maximum delay, making it robust to stragglers in heterogeneous federated learning.","For heterogeneous optimization (e.g., federated learning) this may also be particularly significant. Since delay-adaptive strategies are biased toward faster workers, they do not converge to a stationary point in the heterogeneous data case. Can we use clipping to make training robust to stragglers without introducing a bias?","Specifically, we obtain rates that do not depend on the maximum delay in both the homogeneous and heterogeneous cases. For the heterogeneous case, this is to the best of our knowledge the first asynchronous optimization algorithm that is independent of the maximum delay, suggesting that clipping is beneficial in asynchronous federated learning where severe stragglers are often present.","PAGE A provides the context that federated learning with heterogeneous data suffers from bias with delay-adaptive strategies, and asks if clipping can provide robustness; PAGE B states that Clipped ASGD achieves convergence rates independent of the maximum delay in heterogeneous cases.",pending,multihop-v1,0.431,0.351,needs_both_pages
