2606.13380,2606.13380,"1,2",cross_page,"According to the described agentic system, which components require human involvement, and what is one example of a task that humans are responsible for within this workflow?","The gray-shaded areas indicate the stages that require human involvement, such as selecting task type, and optionally defining constraints; these components are 'Define the task and constraints' and parts of 'Review'.","These components form a closed-loop workflow that combines web-based knowledge acquisition, literature-grounded critique, executable code generation, and experimental feedback.","The gray-shaded areas indicate the stages that require human involvement, such as selecting task type, and optionally defining constraints, whereas the other parts operate fully automatically through computer-based processes.",PAGE A lists and describes the components of the agentic system; PAGE B specifies which components require human involvement and gives an example task.,reject,multihop-v1,0.000,0.756,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.76)"
2606.13380,2606.13380,"2,3",cross_page,"According to the paper, what is the mathematical expression for the quantum kernel used to compare quantum feature maps, and for what central objective is this kernel-based approach evaluated in the QML setting?","The quantum kernel is k(x, x′) = Tr[ρ(x)ρ(x′)], and it is evaluated to achieve performance that competes with or exceeds classical machine learning models.","In the QML setting, a central objective is to achieve performance that com- petes with or exceeds classical machine learning mod- els.","A common choice is the Hilbert–Schmidt inner product k(x, x′) = Tr[ρ(x)ρ(x′)]. (2) This kernel can be used with standard kernel based learn- ing algorithms such as support vector machines or kernel ridge regression [1, 2, 16].","PAGE A provides the central objective for evaluating the kernel-based approach in QML, while PAGE B gives the mathematical expression for the quantum kernel.",pending,multihop-v1,0.000,0.468,needs_both_pages
2606.13380,2606.13380,"3,4",cross_page,"What is the purpose of the 'Discussion' component in the agentic system for automatic quantum circuit generation, and how does it improve computational efficiency compared to previous approaches?","The 'Discussion' component performs lightweight, reasoning-based critique and refinement of candidate ideas, allowing promising designs to be filtered before costly simulation-based validation, thereby improving computational efficiency.","In contrast, our framework targets general-purpose quantum circuit design, rather than task-specific con-structions such as feature maps or VQE ans¨atze, and adopts a language-centric search process in which can-","Furthermore, our approach improves computational efficiency by introducing a “Discussion” component that performs lightweight, reasoning-based critique and refinement of candidate ideas, allowing promising designs to be filtered before costly simulation-based validation.","PAGE A introduces the language-centric search process for general-purpose quantum circuit design, while PAGE B explains that the 'Discussion' component provides reasoning-based critique and refinement to improve computational efficiency.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,1.000,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=1.00)"
2606.13380,2606.13380,"4,5",cross_page,"What information does the 'Generation' component use as input for generating candidate quantum circuit ideas in the first trial, and how is this information produced in the system's workflow?","The 'Generation' component uses the evaluation results of all ideas produced by the 'Exploration' component as input, which are generated through a comprehensive web-based search and subsequent idea seeding and evaluation.","Based on the evaluation results, the number of seed ideas is gradually reduced while assessing the perfor-
mance and characteristics of ideas from various direc-
tions. Through this process, the system selects an initial
idea that will be further refined by the agentic system,
and passes it to the “Generation” component.","For the first
trial, a prompt is designed to instruct the LLM to gener-
ate ideas based on the evaluation results of all ideas pro-
duced by the “Exploration” component.","PAGE A explains that the 'Exploration' component generates and evaluates seed ideas, passing results to 'Generation'; PAGE B states that 'Generation' uses these evaluation results as input for idea generation.",pending,multihop-v1,0.371,0.433,needs_both_pages
2606.13380,2606.13380,"5,6",cross_page,"What is the maximum number of expert questions and final comments allowed per critic round, and how does the system ensure that expert responses are grounded in diverse scientific knowledge?",Up to three expert questions and three final comments are allowed per critic round; expert responses are grounded in diverse scientific knowledge by assigning various expert roles and providing relevant reference materials.,"In this study, up to two rounds of expert questioning were allowed per critic round, and both the number of expert questions and the number of final comments were limited to three.","The LLM serving as the ex- pert is provided with a prompt that includes the expert’s assigned role (e.g., “quantum algorithm benchmarking expert specializing in ansatz expressibility and symmetry effects in VQE optimization”, “quantum chemistry the- orist specializing in electronic structure and symmetry- breaking phenomena”, etc.), the content of the question, and relevant reference materials.",PAGE A gives the numerical limits for expert questions and comments per critic round; PAGE B explains how expert responses are grounded in diverse scientific knowledge via expert roles and reference materials.,reject,multihop-v1,0.647,0.000,"single_page_sufficient(page_A,f1=0.65)"
