2606.13568,2606.13568,"1,2",cross_page,"What is the explicit formula for the scalar output Φ of the adjusted cup-product neural layer when κ = 1, and what is the role of the learned map φθ in this computation?","Φ = ⟨dB + (dA ⌣ A), [M]⟩, where A = φθ(X) is the learned 1-cochain from input features X.","We introduce the adjusted cup-product layer, a neural primitive that hard-wires the cup product together with the adjustment term from higher gauge theory, yielding a readout that is gauge-invariant by construction. Our central theoretical result is a necessity statement: on a closed cycle the layer’s output equals κ⟨dA ⌣A, [M]⟩, so setting the adjustment coefficient κ = 0 annihilates the output identically, independent of all other parameters—the adjustment is the sole source of gauge-invariant signal.","Definition. Given input features X on K, the layer (i) produces a learned 1-cochain A = φθ(X) by a per-simplex map φθ (a small shared MLP on edge features), (ii) forms the curvature F = dA by a fixed sparse coboundary, (iii) forms the adjusted 3-cochain H = dB + κ (F ⌣A), and (iv) returns the scalar Φ = ⟨H, [M]⟩. Only φθ (and an optional affine readout) is learned; the cup, coboundary, and pairing are fixed. The full model uses κ = 1; the ablation κ = 0.","PAGE A explains the necessity of the adjustment term and the output formula involving κ⟨dA ⌣A, [M]⟩, while PAGE B provides the explicit step-by-step construction of Φ and the role of φθ.",pending,multihop-v1,0.000,0.522,needs_both_pages
2606.13568,2606.13568,"2,3",cross_page,"According to the formal theory, what is the output Φ of the AdjustedCupLayer on a closed cycle when the adjustment coefficient κ is set to zero?",The output Φ is identically zero for all A and all B.,The full model uses κ = 1; the ablation κ = 0.,"Theorem 1 (The adjustment is the sole source of signal). For every A, B and every κ, Φκ(A, B) = ⟨dB + κ(dA ⌣A), [M]⟩= κ ⟨dA ⌣A, [M]⟩. In particular, if κ = 0 then Φ0 ≡0 for all A and all B.","PAGE A defines the meaning of κ and mentions the ablation κ = 0, while PAGE B gives the theorem stating that Φ0 ≡ 0 when κ = 0.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,1.000,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=1.00)"
