2606.13629,2606.13629,"1,2",cross_page,"According to the framework described, what is the form of the confidence interval for the real estimand θ* when using synthetic data under task exchangeability, and how is the gap used to expand the naive interval determined?","The confidence interval is [˜L − ˆ∆, ˜U + ˆ∆], where ˆ∆ is the (1 − β)-quantile of the historical discrepancies between synthetic and real estimands from related tasks.","The key insight is a new technical condition that we call task exchangeability. Informally, this is a requirement that the researcher can identify historical tasks, for which real data is available, such that their current task of interest is exchangeable with the historical tasks in an appropriate mathematical sense.","Denoting this empirical quantile by
ˆ∆= Q1−β (∆1,...,∆T ),
we can form the expanded interval
CI = [˜L −ˆ∆, ˜U + ˆ∆].",PAGE A defines task exchangeability and the need for historical tasks with real data; PAGE B gives the explicit formula for the confidence interval and how the gap ˆ∆ is determined from historical discrepancies.,reject,multihop-v1,0.000,0.686,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.69)"
2606.13629,2606.13629,"2,3",cross_page,"According to the framework described, how is the empirical quantile ˆ∆ used to construct a confidence interval for the true estimand θ∗ in the context of ANES feeling-thermometer scores, and what does this adjustment address compared to the naive synthetic-only interval?","The empirical quantile ˆ∆ is used to expand the naive synthetic-data interval by [−ˆ∆, ˆ∆], yielding a wider confidence interval that accounts for the real–synthetic gap, thus providing coverage for θ∗ at the desired rate and correcting the bias and narrowness of the naive interval.","Denoting this empirical quantile by
ˆ∆= Q1−β (∆1,...,∆T ),
we can form the expanded interval
CI = [˜L −ˆ∆, ˜U + ˆ∆].","For each task, we compare the naive synthetic-only interval, which treats the synthetic data as real data,
with the interval obtained via task exchangeability. We set the target overall coverage level to be 0.85.
We treat survey results from the previous survey wave, reporting analogous feeling thermometer scores,
as the historical tasks exchangeable with the one of interest. The synthetic-only intervals are too narrow
and severely biased, missing the true estimand θ∗. This is consistent with the findings of Bisbee et al.,
who observe that language model responses exhibit less variation than real surveys. By contrast, the task-
exchangeability intervals are wider, reflecting uncertainty about the real–synthetic gap, and cover the true
values at the desired rate.","PAGE A defines how ˆ∆ is used to expand the confidence interval, while PAGE B describes the application to ANES scores and explains the effect of this adjustment compared to the naive interval.",pending,multihop-v1,0.545,0.543,needs_both_pages
2606.13629,2606.13629,"3,4",cross_page,"What is the key assumption that allows the authors to calibrate the real–synthetic estimation gap for a new task without using any real data from that task, and how is this assumption operationalized in their inference procedure?","Task exchangeability is the key assumption, allowing calibration of the real–synthetic gap using historical tasks; it is operationalized by treating the current and historical tasks as related draws from a larger population and using this to form coverage guarantees.","We treat survey results from the previous survey wave, reporting analogous feeling thermometer scores, as the historical tasks exchangeable with the one of interest.",task exchangeability serving as the condition that makes this calibration valid. Our framework can be extended to settings where some target-task real data is available; we discuss this case and the connection to [7] in Section 4.4.,"PAGE A provides the operational use of exchangeable historical tasks, while PAGE B explicitly states that task exchangeability is the condition enabling calibration without real data from the target task.",pending,multihop-v1,0.000,0.509,needs_both_pages
2606.13629,2606.13629,"4,5",cross_page,"According to the paper, what is the definition of a 'task' and how is the synthetic-data target for a task denoted?","A task is defined as a functional–distribution pair T = (θ, P), and the synthetic-data target for a task is denoted as ˜θ = θ(P̃).","Let θ(P) ∈Θ denote a statistical target, defined by applying a functional θ(·) to a data distribution P. The set Θ is a space of possible quantities of interest, such as means, medians, regression coefficients, and other population-level summaries. We refer to a functional–distribution pair T = (θ,P) as a task.","We denote this synthetic distribution by ˜P = GT ∗. For example, ˜P could be the dis- tribution of “silicon samples,” obtained by prompting a language model to mimic responses from a target demographic population. We then observe a synthetic dataset ˜S = { ˜X1,..., ˜XN} i.i.d. ∼ ˜P. The corresponding synthetic-data target is ˜θ = θ∗( ˜P).","PAGE A provides the definition of a 'task' as a functional–distribution pair, while PAGE B explains how the synthetic-data target for a task is denoted.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,0.947,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.95)"
