2606.13629,2606.13629,"14,15",cross_page,"According to the methodology described, what is the two-dimensional estimand being inferred for each task in the Pew ATP presidential approval experiment, and how is the synthetic estimand ˜θj for a cell constructed?",The two-dimensional estimand is the average approval among co-partisan and opposition respondents for each field date and census region; the synthetic estimand ˜θj is the weighted mean of four independent GPT-4o-generated approval indicators per respondent.,"For every panelist we query GPT-4o through the API, supplying the respondent’s demographic and political profile (census region, age, gender, education, race and ethnicity, religion, income, and party identification) and recording the model’s approve/disapprove answer. We take four independent generations per respondent and average them to form a synthetic approval indicator; the synthetic estimand ˜θj is the weighted mean of these indicators, and the gap ∆j = θj −˜θj measures GPT-4o’s simulation bias for cell j.","Figure 3: Inference on presidential approval based on Pew ATP surveys via task exchangeability. Each row corresponds to a field date and census region, with the two-dimensional estimand equal to the average approval among respondents who share the president’s party (left column) and the average approval among respondents from the opposing party (right column).","PAGE A explains how the synthetic estimand ˜θj is constructed using GPT-4o generations, while PAGE B defines the two-dimensional estimand as average approval among co-partisan and opposition respondents for each field date and region.",pending,multihop-v1,0.519,0.485,needs_both_pages
2606.13634,2606.13634,"1,2",cross_page,"According to the paper, what is the name of the operad defined where operations correspond to question templates and composition corresponds to substitution of sub-answers, and what is the formal definition of an operad that this structure satisfies?","The questions operad Q; a (non-unital) operad O is a collection O(0), O(1), O(2), ... of sets with composition maps ◦i : O(k) × O(ℓ) → O(k + ℓ−1) satisfying sequential and parallel associativity.","We define the questions operad Q, in which operations correspond to question templates and composition corresponds to substitution of sub-answers, and show how QA models can be interpreted as algebras over Q.","Definition 2.1. A (non-unital) operad O is the following data: • A collection O(0), O(1), O(2), . . . of sets. (Elements of O(k) are thought of as arity-k morphisms, i.e. operations with k inputs and one output.) • For every k, ℓ≥0 and 1 ≤i ≤k, a “composition map” ◦i : O(k) × O(ℓ) →O(k + ℓ−1). (When O is literally a collection of morphisms, and we are given f ∈O(k) and g ∈O(ℓ), f ◦ig is obtained by plugging the output of g into the i-th input slot of f. The result is a (k + ℓ−1)-ary operation.) These data must satisfy the following conditions: • Composition is sequentially associative. That is, for every k, ℓ, m ≥0, f ∈O(k), g ∈O(ℓ), h ∈O(m), and 1 ≤i ≤k, 1 ≤j ≤ℓ, the compositions f ◦i (g ◦j h), (f ◦i g) ◦i+j−1 h (3) agree. (Compare (2).) • Composition into disjoint slots commutes (parallel associativity). For f ∈O(k), g ∈O(ℓ), h ∈O(m), and 1 ≤i < j ≤k, the compositions (f ◦i g) ◦j+ℓ−1 h, (f ◦j h) ◦i g (4) agree. (The shifted index j + ℓ−1 accounts for g’s ℓ blanks displacing the j-th slot of f.)","PAGE A provides the name and application of the questions operad Q, while PAGE B gives the formal definition of an operad that Q satisfies.",pending,multihop-v1,0.286,0.000,needs_both_pages
2606.13634,2606.13634,"2,3",cross_page,"According to the definition of a colored operad provided, what is the set DG(B1, ..., Bk; A) in the context of a context-free grammar G, and how is composition defined in DG?","DG(B1, ..., Bk; A) consists of derivation trees for G whose k leaves have labels B1, ..., Bk and whose root has label A; composition is by grafting the root of one tree to a leaf of another, matching labels.","If O is a colored operad, we denote by O(c1, . . . , ck; d) the morphisms that take k inputs with colors c1, . . . , ck, respectively, and return an output of color d.","DG(B1, . . . , Bk; A) consists of the derivation trees for G whose k leaves have labels B1, . . . , Bk and whose root has label A. • Composition in DG is given by grafting the root of one derivation tree to a leaf of another — a common operation in context-free parsing — subject to the requirement that the labels at the two nodes being identified must match.","PAGE A provides the general definition of colored operads and notation O(c1, ..., ck; d); PAGE B specifies what DG(B1, ..., Bk; A) means for a context-free grammar and how composition works.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,0.883,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.88)"
2606.13634,2606.13634,"3,4",cross_page,"According to the operadic framework described, what is the set LG(A) for a non-terminal A in a context-free grammar G, and how does the operad DG relate to it?","LG(A) is the language generated by A, i.e. the language of the subgrammar with A as the start symbol, and DG is the operad of derivation trees for G whose algebras include LG.","We define DG to be the colored operad of derivation trees with non-terminal leaves for G, with colors V . That is: • Given B1, . . . , Bk ∈ V and A ∈ V , DG(B1, . . . , Bk; A) consists of the derivation trees for G whose k leaves have labels B1, . . . , Bk and whose root has label A.","• For A ∈V , LG(A) is the language generated by A, i.e. the language of the subgrammar with A as the start symbol.","PAGE A defines the operad DG in terms of derivation trees for G, while PAGE B defines LG(A) as the language generated by A and explains that LG is an algebra over DG.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,0.793,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.79)"
2606.13634,2606.13634,"4,5",cross_page,"According to the operadic approach described, what is the ambiguity algebra for a context-free grammar G, and how is it constructed from the operad DG and its associated algebras?","The ambiguity algebra is the kernel ker φ of the forgetful map φ: F R_G → L R_G, where F R_G is the DR_G-algebra of linearized complete derivation trees with leaves labeled by terminals, and L R_G is the linearized yield algebra.","Our reformulation of a CFG as an operad DG and an algebra LG enables us to define a new avatar of ambiguity, called the ambiguity algebra ker φ. This is a canonical object that recovers the usual definition of ambiguity as the condition ker φ ̸= 0, while additionally encoding the compositional structure of ambiguity. To define the ambiguity algebra, we first linearize DG and LG to form DR_G and LR_G. That is, we define DR_G(B1, . . . , Bk; A) to consist of formal R-linear combinations of elements of DG(B1, . . . , Bk; A), and LR_G(A) similarly. LR_G is then an algebra over DR_G. We can define a","second DR_G-algebra, F R_G, which is the linearization of the DG-algebra FG consisting of those complete derivation trees whose leaves are all labeled by terminals, and where the DG-action is defined by grafting. There is a forgetful map, φ: F R_G →LR_G, which sends a derivation tree to its yield. We now define the ambiguity algebra to be the kernel ker φ, i.e. those elements of F R_G that are sent to zero by φ.","PAGE A defines the ambiguity algebra concept and the linearization process for DG and LG; PAGE B specifies the construction of F R_G, the forgetful map φ, and the formal definition of the ambiguity algebra as ker φ.",pending,multihop-v1,0.267,0.571,needs_both_pages
2606.13637,2606.13637,"1,2",cross_page,"What is the approximate value and standard deviation of the recovery subspace dimensionality kt for the penultimate layer of ResNet-18 trained sequentially on Split CIFAR-100, and how is kt formally defined?","kt is approximately 8 with standard deviation σ = 0.82, defined as the minimum rank of the linear probe weight matrix Wt such that the rank-k approximation preserves at least 90% of full probe accuracy.","The recovery subspace dimensionality kt remains constant at approximately 8 across all tasks (σ = 0.82), falsifying our original hypothesis of Recoverability Diffusion.",Recovery Subspace Dimensionality (kt).  The minimum rank of the linear probe weight matrix Wt such that the rank-k approximation preserves at least 90% of full probe accuracy.,"PAGE A provides the empirical value and standard deviation of kt, while PAGE B gives the formal definition of kt.",reject,multihop-v1,0.304,0.862,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.86)"
