2606.13637,2606.13637,"4,5",cross_page,"What is the mean value of the recovery subspace dimensionality kt as a percentage of the total representational dimensionality, and what does this imply about ResNet-18's capacity to maintain a stable recovery manifold?","The mean kt is 8.0, which is approximately 1.56% of the total representational dimensionality (8/512), implying that ResNet-18 maintains a stable low-dimensional recovery manifold regardless of task count.",TABLE IV.  Geometric drift from Task 0 checkpoint to final model.,"kt fluctuates within {7, 8, 9} with mean 8.0 and σ = 0.82, occupying ≈1.56% of total representational dimensionality (8/512). This stability has three key implications: (1) ResNet-18 has an intrinsic capacity to maintain a stable low-dimensional recovery manifold regardless of task count; (2) recovery does not become harder over time; and (3) a targeted intervention preserving only 8 singular directions is sufficient at t = 0 and remains sufficient at t = 9.","PAGE A provides the context of representational dimensionality (512) and experiment numbering, while PAGE B gives the mean kt value, its percentage, and the implication for ResNet-18's capacity.",reject,multihop-v1,0.000,0.750,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.75)"
2606.13637,2606.13637,"5,6",cross_page,"According to the regression model for recoverability Rt, what is the coefficient for the geometric predictor that is also identified as the strongest single predictor by Pearson correlation, and what is its value?",The coefficient for Dt (principal-angle drift) is 0.001.,"Dt increases monotonically, making it a natural geometric clock for the forgetting process.","Rt = 0.455 · LPt + 0.237 · PEt + 0.001 · Dt + 0.207,   R² = 0.8222
More than 82% of the variance in recoverability is explained by three geometric variables. Recoverability is geometrically determined, not random. Dt is the strongest single predictor (r = −0.862), not LPt.","PAGE A defines Dt as principal-angle drift and describes its monotonic increase, while PAGE B provides the regression model showing Dt's coefficient and identifies Dt as the strongest single predictor.",pending,multihop-v1,0.000,0.385,needs_both_pages
2606.13663,2606.13663,"1,2",cross_page,"What is the improvement in average accuracy on Qwen3-8B when using HyperTool-format trajectories for supervised fine-tuning, and what specific change in context management does HyperTool introduce compared to prior methods?","HyperTool-format trajectories improve average accuracy on Qwen3-8B from 9.93% to 33.33%, and HyperTool shifts context management from post-hoc trace compression to execution-time control by folding tool operations into a single code block.","On MCP-Universe, HyperTool improves average accuracy from 15.69% to 35.29% on Qwen3-32B and from 9.93% to 33.33% on Qwen3-8B, and surpass GPT-OSS and Kimi-k2.5 on average accuracy, showing that our HyperTool can substantially improve multi-step tool use.","To move context management from post-hoc trace compression to execution-time control, we propose HyperTool, a unified MCP-compatible executable interface whose input is a code block. Inside the block, existing tools can be invoked through their original schemas, while intermediate outputs can be stored, transformed, filtered, and passed across calls using ordinary program logic.","PAGE A provides the quantitative improvement in accuracy for Qwen3-8B, while PAGE B explains the specific change in context management introduced by HyperTool.",pending,multihop-v1,0.514,0.548,needs_both_pages
2606.13663,2606.13663,"2,3",cross_page,"What is the main difference between the execution granularity of tool use in the standard MCP-style interface and in HyperTool, and how does HyperTool achieve this difference?","The standard MCP-style interface exposes each tool operation as an atomic, model-visible transition, while HyperTool allows multiple dependent tool calls to be executed inside a single block, returning only one observation to the main trace by folding locally deterministic tool chains into one executable block.","To move context management from post-hoc trace compression to execution-time control, we propose HyperTool, a unified MCP-compatible executable interface whose input is a code block. Inside the block, existing tools can be invoked through their original schemas, while intermedi- ate outputs can be stored, transformed, filtered, and passed across calls using ordinary program logic. This unified format supports both atomic tool calls and multi-tool subroutines within the same inter- face. By shifting local execution into the block, HyperTool improves context management and tool- use expressivity at the same time: transient observa- tions and deterministic state transfer stay out of the main trace, while code can compose tools, trans- form intermediate values, and define temporary helper functions.","Under the standard MCP-style interface, each action is an atomic tool call: at = Call(τt, xt), τt ∈T , where τt is the selected tool and xt is its input. Thus, a workflow with k dependent tool operations must be expressed as k model-visible transitions. This atomic execution granularity is simple and general, but it exposes every intermediate observa- tion, value transfer, and procedural step to the main reasoning trace. ... HyperTool changes the model-visible unit of tool execution. Instead of requiring every MCP-style tool operation to appear as a separate transition, HyperTool exposes a unified executable interface: at = Block(St), where St is a bounded executable program writ- ten over the primitive tools in T . The HyperTool runtime executes St, allows existing tools to be in- voked through their original schemas, and returns a single block-level observation ot to the outer trace.","PAGE A describes HyperTool's block-based execution and its ability to group tool calls, while PAGE B defines the standard MCP-style atomic tool call interface and explicitly contrasts it with HyperTool's approach.",reject,multihop-v1,0.302,0.688,"single_page_sufficient(page_B,f1=0.69)"
2606.13663,2606.13663,"3,4",cross_page,"According to the HyperTool framework, what is the model-visible unit of tool execution, and how does this differ from the standard MCP-style interface in terms of what is returned to the main reasoning trace?","The model-visible unit of tool execution in HyperTool is a block (Block(St)), which returns only a single block-level observation to the main reasoning trace, unlike the standard MCP-style interface that exposes every intermediate observation as separate model-visible transitions.","HyperTool changes the model-visible unit of tool execution. Instead of requiring every MCP-style tool operation to appear as a separate transition, HyperTool exposes a unified executable interface: at = Block(St), where St is a bounded executable program written over the primitive tools in T . The HyperTool runtime executes St, allows existing tools to be in- voked through their original schemas, and returns a single block-level observation ot to the outer trace.","Only the final block-level observation is returned to the main reasoning trace. can remain inside the executable block. In con- trast, observations that may change the task plan, reveal an unknown schema, invalidate the current direction, or require semantic judgment should be returned to the main reasoning trace. Thus, Hyper- Tool does not replace the underlying MCP tools; it changes which parts of tool execution are exposed as separate model-visible transitions.","PAGE A defines the HyperTool block as the new model-visible unit and describes its execution, while PAGE B clarifies that only the final block-level observation is returned to the main reasoning trace, contrasting with the standard MCP-style interface.",reject,multihop-v1,0.693,0.609,"single_page_sufficient(page_A,f1=0.69)"
